\title{Example 3.2: sires nested in breed, heritability and breed comparisons}
\description{
Weaning weights of lambs (\code{\link{ex32}}) with sex, age and breed fixed and sires within breed random: the
heritability \eqn{h^2 = 4\sigma_s^2/(\sigma_s^2 + \sigma^2)}, Tukey-Kramer comparisons of the breeds and the
predicted breeding values of the sires. The book prints only part of the data, so the numbers differ from those
of the book (for this part the sire variance is estimated as zero).
}
\examples{
# SAS program of the book (Appendix B):
#   proc mixed data=ex32;
#     class sex sire_id breed;
#     model ww = sex agew breed / solution ddfm=satterth;
#     random sire_id(breed) / solution;
#     lsmeans breed / adjust=tukey;
#   run;

fm <-
  vlm_fit(
      fixed    = Ww ~ sex + agew + breed
    , data     = ex32
    , random   = ~ breed:sire_id
    , repeated = NULL
    , method   = "REML"
    , ddfm     = "satterth"
    , type     = 3
    , class    = "breed"
    , bound    = TRUE
    , control  = list()
    )
v <- fm$covariance$estimate
h2 <- 4 * v[1] / sum(v)
h2
vlm_lsmeans(
    fit    = fm
  , specs  = ~ breed
  , at     = NULL
  , diff   = TRUE
  , adjust = "tukey"
  , level  = 0.95
  )
vlm_blup(
    fit   = fm
  , level = 0.95
  )
}
\references{
Duchateau, L., Janssen, P. and Rowlands, G. J. (1998). \emph{Linear Mixed Models. An Introduction with
